content="<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to fix this Ansible Playbook that's failing ansible-lint. Let me look at the errors first.\n\nThe first error is about the command module not accepting inline environment variables. Oh right, in Ansible, the command module doesn't support environment variables the same way as other modules. So using 'ansible.builtin.command' with 'cmd' and 'changed_when' is causing an issue because it's using an older approach.\n\nThe second error is about the 'changed_when' parameter being set to false, which is not recommended because it can hide errors. The linting rule says commands shouldn't change things if nothing needs doing, so using 'changed_when' here is problematic.\n\nLooking at the tasks, the second task is using 'ansible.builtin.command' to run 'helm version'. I remember that using shell commands directly in Ansible isn't the best practice, especially for version checks. Instead, there's a module called 'helm_version' from the 'helm' collection that's designed for this purpose. This module would handle the version check more cleanly and avoid the linting issues.\n\nSo, I'll replace the command module with the 'helm.helm_version' module. This way, I eliminate the need for inline environment variables and the 'changed_when' parameter. Plus, it's more idempotent and aligns with best practices.\n\nI also need to make sure the variable 'helm_version' is correctly passed. Since the download task uses this variable, I should ensure it's defined elsewhere, maybe in a vars file or as a parameter.\n\nSo, the corrected task for checking Helm version will use 'helm.helm_version' with 'binary_path' set to '/usr/local/bin/helm'. This should fix both linting issues and improve the task's reliability.\n\nI won't change the other tasks because they don't have linting errors. The first task checks if Helm exists using 'stat', which is fine, and the third task downloads Helm using 'unarchive', which is also correct.\n\nBy making these changes, the playbook should now pass ansible-lint without altering the original functionality or task order.\n</think>\n\nTo fix the Ansible Playbook and resolve the linting errors, I made the following changes:\n\n1. **Replaced `ansible.builtin.command` with `helm.helm_version` module** to properly check the Helm version without using inline environment variables or `changed_when`.\n2. **Removed the `changed_when` parameter** as it's not needed with the new module.\n\nHere's the corrected playbook:\n\n```yaml\n---\n- name: Check if Helm binary exists\n  ansible.builtin.stat:\n    path: /usr/local/bin/helm\n\n- name: Check Helm version\n  helm.helm_version:\n    binary_path: /usr/local/bin/helm\n\n- name: Download Helm\n  ansible.builtin.unarchive:\n    remote_src: true\n    url: https://get.helm.sh/helm-{{ helm_version }}-linux-amd64.tar.gz\n    dest: /tmp\n```" additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={'model': 'deepseek-r1:14b', 'created_at': '2025-10-12T09:11:45.179967851Z', 'done': True, 'done_reason': 'stop', 'total_duration': 13285590075, 'load_duration': 81805685, 'prompt_eval_count': 1289, 'prompt_eval_duration': 490193780, 'eval_count': 627, 'eval_duration': 12712871847, 'model_name': 'deepseek-r1:14b'} id='run--6dca964f-51bb-4493-9db4-9dfa5297a12c-0' usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1289, 'output_tokens': 627, 'total_tokens': 1916}
